#%PAM-1.0
#
# Marathon Shell PAM Configuration
#
# - pam_fprintd is not in the auth chain. Marathon's SecurityManager calls
#   fprintd over D-Bus directly when biometric auth is selected, and having
#   pam_fprintd here would consume the password token from the conversation
#   callback on devices without a fingerprint reader, breaking pam_unix.
#
# - pam_faillock is not in the auth chain either. pam_faillock writes per-
#   user tally files under /run/faillock; the shell runs as the logged-in
#   user with only CAP_SYS_NICE, so it has no permission to create or
#   update those files, and the "required preauth" step fails the entire
#   stack with PAM_AUTH_ERR regardless of whether the password is right.
#   Marathon's SecurityManager implements its own lockout / rate limiting
#   (failedAttempts, isLockedOut, lockoutSecondsRemaining) which is what
#   protects the shell at user level.

# Authentication: just check the password against pam_unix
auth    sufficient  pam_unix.so nullok
auth    required    pam_deny.so

# Account validation
account required    pam_unix.so

# Session management - integrate with systemd-logind
session required    pam_systemd.so
session required    pam_unix.so
session optional    pam_gnome_keyring.so auto_start

# Password management (for future password change functionality)
password required   pam_unix.so sha512 shadow nullok
